1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,848
A developer on a locked-down
PC wants an AI pair programmer.

2
00:00:04,848 --> 00:00:07,221
Cloud assistants are blocked,

3
00:00:07,221 --> 00:00:11,394
and nobody wants project
files leaving the machine.

4
00:00:11,394 --> 00:00:15,162
Phoenix LAi runs Gemma
models on your own GPU,

5
00:00:15,162 --> 00:00:19,258
serves them as an OpenAI API,
and gives you a chat

6
00:00:19,258 --> 00:00:23,272
that edits project files
only where you allow it.

7
00:00:23,272 --> 00:00:26,828
Everything you will see is running
for real.

8
00:00:27,078 --> 00:00:30,004
Phoenix is two programs on one PC.

9
00:00:30,004 --> 00:00:34,887
The server turns Gemma 4 models
into an OpenAI API on port 1234,

10
00:00:34,887 --> 00:00:37,252
with a dashboard and a console.

11
00:00:37,744 --> 00:00:39,279
Models are plain files in a models folder.

12
00:00:39,979 --> 00:00:42,642
The engine runs them on your GPU
through the driver's own Vulkan.

13
00:00:43,358 --> 00:00:46,682
No installs, no admin.

14
00:00:47,471 --> 00:00:50,462
The client is the chat window.

15
00:00:50,462 --> 00:00:53,935
It holds your project
files in a jailed folder,

16
00:00:53,935 --> 00:00:59,477
with a backup before every edit,
and talks to the server over the same API.

17
00:01:00,177 --> 00:01:02,901
Providers are any OpenAI
compatible address:

18
00:01:02,901 --> 00:01:06,739
the Phoenix server, Fairlead,
LM Studio, Ollama, even a cloud.

19
00:01:06,739 --> 00:01:10,524
Only the Phoenix server
gets your files by default.

20
00:01:10,524 --> 00:01:12,366
Everyone else gets what you type.

21
00:01:13,127 --> 00:01:17,462
Cloud keys live in Lazarette
and are shared to Fairlead.

22
00:01:17,462 --> 00:01:19,893
Phoenix never reads the vault.

23
00:01:19,893 --> 00:01:24,562
It holds only its own Fairlead key,
in Windows Credential Manager.

24
00:01:25,262 --> 00:01:27,981
A VS Code panel shows the same chat,

25
00:01:27,981 --> 00:01:30,925
with inline completion from the server.

26
00:01:31,625 --> 00:01:34,838
And the safety line:
the model never runs a command,

27
00:01:34,838 --> 00:01:38,854
every edit keeps the old version,
and your files stay on this PC.

28
00:01:38,854 --> 00:01:41,840
This is the Phoenix server's dashboard,
live.

29
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:45,047
No model is loaded
and no GPU memory is in use.

30
00:01:45,047 --> 00:01:48,612
One client is connected,
the Phoenix client,

31
00:01:48,612 --> 00:01:50,719
with its last call listed.

32
00:01:50,719 --> 00:01:52,063
Everything here is local.

33
00:01:52,763 --> 00:01:57,334
The console speaks only Phoenix's
own commands, never a shell.

34
00:01:57,334 --> 00:02:00,150
Status shows the API address
and the device.

35
00:02:00,850 --> 00:02:02,847
Models lists what the API offers.

36
00:02:02,847 --> 00:02:04,482
Here, no models folder yet.

37
00:02:05,346 --> 00:02:06,346
This PC has no Gemma 4 file,
and Phoenix refuses other models.

38
00:02:06,366 --> 00:02:08,513
So for this demo,

39
00:02:08,513 --> 00:02:12,681
the model comes through Fairlead.

40
00:02:12,931 --> 00:02:18,322
The client opens, and the Fleet
skin switches it to navy and brass.

41
00:02:18,322 --> 00:02:21,621
Providers lists the Phoenix server,

42
00:02:21,621 --> 00:02:24,260
green because it is running.

43
00:02:24,260 --> 00:02:25,660
Now we add Fairlead.

44
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:30,053
Plus. A name, Fairlead's local address,

45
00:02:30,053 --> 00:02:34,149
and the key Fairlead issued to this app.

46
00:02:34,149 --> 00:02:39,565
It goes straight into Windows
Credential Manager, never into a file.

47
00:02:40,419 --> 00:02:45,180
Load models: Fairlead offers its routes,
here one called local.

48
00:02:45,180 --> 00:02:47,689
Test probes eighteen OpenAI features,

49
00:02:47,689 --> 00:02:51,620
so Phoenix never sends this
provider something it refuses.

50
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:56,053
The feature map is saved
with the provider.

51
00:02:56,053 --> 00:03:01,154
Embeddings are not offered,
so docs search falls back to keywords.

52
00:03:01,154 --> 00:03:04,955
And look at the privacy switch: off.

53
00:03:04,955 --> 00:03:10,539
This provider gets only what you type,
never your files.

54
00:03:10,539 --> 00:03:11,539
Save.

55
00:03:11,637 --> 00:03:14,983
First live answer:
the question goes through Fairlead

56
00:03:14,983 --> 00:03:20,096
to a three billion parameter Llama on this
machine, about ninety tokens a second.

57
00:03:20,096 --> 00:03:25,618
The answer is rough, as small models are,
but it is real and it is live.

58
00:03:26,230 --> 00:03:29,812
Scenario: a ledger clerk
with a small PowerShell script.

59
00:03:29,812 --> 00:03:33,835
A project is a folder,
jailed inside Phoenix projects.

60
00:03:33,835 --> 00:03:35,957
I drop in two synthetic files,

61
00:03:35,957 --> 00:03:39,846
a script and a sample ledger,
and ask about the script.

62
00:03:40,472 --> 00:03:44,717
With the privacy switch off,
Fairlead never saw the file.

63
00:03:44,717 --> 00:03:47,737
The model can only guess, and it does.

64
00:03:47,737 --> 00:03:50,611
That is the gate working, not a bug.

65
00:03:51,311 --> 00:03:52,311
To let

66
00:03:52,311 --> 00:03:56,037
this provider see the project, open its
window and switch on send local context.

67
00:03:56,037 --> 00:04:00,409
The red warning says project
files are sent to this provider.

68
00:04:00,409 --> 00:04:04,288
Fairlead's local route stays on this PC,
so I accept it.

69
00:04:04,288 --> 00:04:09,271
Save. Now the same chat lists the project
with a real list command,

70
00:04:09,271 --> 00:04:12,560
and finds the script,
the ledger and a rules file.

71
00:04:13,260 --> 00:04:16,835
A deliberately mechanical edit
for a small model:

72
00:04:16,835 --> 00:04:19,754
replace this text with the decimal cast.

73
00:04:19,754 --> 00:04:22,443
Phoenix shows the diff, writes it,

74
00:04:22,443 --> 00:04:27,032
and keeps the previous version
in a dot llm backup folder.

75
00:04:27,032 --> 00:04:31,052
A small model can get this wrong,
so the backup is the point.

76
00:04:31,384 --> 00:04:35,086
Safety. I ask the model to run the script.

77
00:04:35,086 --> 00:04:40,455
It cannot: Phoenix never runs a command,
so all it can do is answer in text.

78
00:04:40,885 --> 00:04:43,564
I ask again, this time with permission.

79
00:04:43,564 --> 00:04:47,811
It still declines,
and offers a script for me to run myself.

80
00:04:48,127 --> 00:04:50,334
A third try, and the run guard steps in.

81
00:04:50,334 --> 00:04:53,667
Chat is suspended for five minutes,
with a countdown,

82
00:04:53,667 --> 00:04:56,497
and by design the lockout
survives a restart.

83
00:04:56,497 --> 00:04:59,566
That is a safety stop, not a bug.

84
00:05:00,266 --> 00:05:04,577
The project jail: I try
to open a folder outside Phoenix projects.

85
00:05:04,577 --> 00:05:06,763
Dots in the path are refused,

86
00:05:06,763 --> 00:05:07,763
and the

87
00:05:07,763 --> 00:05:13,471
message says folders are always inside the
projects folder, fixed and unchangeable.

88
00:05:14,171 --> 00:05:17,900
Same answer for a project path
that tries to climb out with dot dot.

89
00:05:17,900 --> 00:05:21,786
Everything in the client is
a slash command, no menus.

90
00:05:21,786 --> 00:05:25,629
Help lists them all:
connect a project, switch provider,

91
00:05:25,629 --> 00:05:29,815
compact a long chat, accept mode
to approve each file action.

92
00:05:30,515 --> 00:05:33,730
Skills are reusable
instructions in plain files,

93
00:05:33,730 --> 00:05:36,610
matched to what you type
or called by name.

94
00:05:36,610 --> 00:05:39,559
The Skill Builder drafts and tests them.

95
00:05:39,809 --> 00:05:41,315
The evidence trail.

96
00:05:41,315 --> 00:05:42,984
The chat is saved with the project,

97
00:05:42,984 --> 00:05:43,984
and the edit

98
00:05:43,984 --> 00:05:47,181
line names the exact backup folder holding
the version from before the change.

99
00:05:47,881 --> 00:05:50,790
Open the saved chat
and every step is there:

100
00:05:50,790 --> 00:05:54,095
the list, the diff,
the write and the backup path.

101
00:05:54,795 --> 00:05:55,795
Back

102
00:05:55,795 --> 00:05:58,680
on the server dashboard, the only requests
are the client's embedding checks.

103
00:05:58,680 --> 00:06:01,059
The chat itself went through Fairlead.

104
00:06:01,559 --> 00:06:05,169
Phoenix LAi keeps the work on the machine.

105
00:06:05,169 --> 00:06:08,207
Your files go only where you allow,

106
00:06:08,207 --> 00:06:13,764
the model never runs a command,
and every edit keeps its backup.

107
00:06:13,764 --> 00:06:16,901
Today's model came through Fairlead,

108
00:06:16,901 --> 00:06:20,038
because this PC has no Gemma 4 file.

109
00:06:20,038 --> 00:06:22,874
Everything else you saw was live.
